{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Jacob was left alone.</b> The last of them all to depart from the camp, in order to guide all his people so that they would go with all his possessions and nothing would remain in the camp." "<b>And a man wrestled with him.</b> This was the act of an angel, without doubt, by command of his Creator — in the manner of יְרֵה וַיּוֹר (II Kings 13:17) — so that Jacob and his seed would be delivered by YHWH, even though there would be loss of property, which is the יְקוּם that is in their feet (Deuteronomy 25:11), and with this the deliverance would ultimately come with blessing."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "Sforno reads the phrase יְרֵה וַיּוֹר from II Kings 13:17 as the model for symbolic prophetic action performed by an angel at divine command. The phrase יְקוּם אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלֵיהֶם alludes to Deuteronomy 25:11, where יְקוּם denotes another's property/rights; Sforno leaves the Hebrew term untranslated as it carries the legal-philosophical specificity of what is owed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:33:05.757095919Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}